<h2>Your HRV in context</h2>
<p><strong>Cortisol+ is an Apple Watch stress tracking app.</strong> It brings your available heart rate variability (HRV), resting heart rate, sleep and activity into one view.</p>
<p>HRV is the small change in time between your heartbeats.</p>
<p>The app's wellness score sums up how it reads those signals. It is not a cortisol measurement.</p>

<h2>What can you do in the app?</h2>
<ul>
  <li>See which body signals are adding to your current score.</li>
  <li>Compare your available readings with your history.</li>
  <li>Look at sleep and activity next to your stress and recovery trends.</li>
  <li>Use guided breathing, and note how you feel before and after.</li>
</ul>
<p>Core analysis needs an active subscription or an eligible trial.</p>
<p>Downloading the app is free. The in-app offer shows the current price, how often it renews and the trial terms.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_presenting_new.webp">
  Wear your watch <strong>the same way each day</strong>, including to bed. Steady habits make your trends easier to read.
</ZenTip>

<h2>What does HRV mean here?</h2>
<p><strong>Apple Watch reports HRV as SDNN.</strong> SDNN is one way of measuring how much the gaps between heartbeats vary.</p>
<p>The app compares that value with your baseline, alongside other signals.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5900369/">research on HRV and stress</a> describes how the nervous system responds across many studies. It does not show that a person's hormone level can be worked out from one HRV reading.</p>

<h3>Why numbers from different devices don't always match</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Where the data comes from, and how it was recorded, both matter.</li>
  <li>SDNN and RMSSD are different calculations. A number from one device can't always be compared directly with another.</li>
  <li>Background readings are taken now and then. They are not a nonstop stream of data, and they are not cortisol readings.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How does it learn your normal?</h2>
<p><strong>The baseline draws on your readings from the past 30 days.</strong> It starts with population defaults, begins blending in your own history after 10 stored readings, and is fully personal at 28 readings. That's a count of readings, not calendar days.</p>
<p>Which signals are available, and how recent they are, affect the result.</p>
<p>The <a href="/how-it-works/">methodology page</a> lists the actual weights, baseline rules and limits.</p>

